    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/soria"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/soria.jpg" alt="Soria" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/soria">Soria</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soria_-_P7234619_edited.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FRANCIS RAHER, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Celtiberians were a group of Celtic-speaking peoples of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meseta-central">Meseta</a>, the high plateau of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, in the upper basins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a>'s southern tributaries, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a>, roughly the modern provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soria">Soria</a>, Guadalajara, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teruel">Teruel</a> and Cuenca. They were never a single state but a cluster of related peoples, the most important being the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arevaci">Arevaci</a>, Belli, Titti and Lusones, with the Pellendones sometimes counted among them. Ancient writers explained their name as a mixture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a>, but modern scholars treat it as a geographical and cultural label coined by Greek and Roman observers. Archaeologically, a distinct culture can be traced from about the seventh and sixth centuries BCE, when cemeteries of cremation burials with iron weapons and hilltop settlements spread across the region, apparently developing from local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> communities rather than from a single migration. How and when Celtic speech reached the peninsula is still debated. Over the following centuries the scattered hillforts grew into larger fortified towns, and by the third century BCE the Celtiberians lived in a landscape of oppida such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numantia">Numantia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiermes">Termes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uxama-argaela">Uxama</a> among the Arevaci, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segeda">Segeda</a> among the Belli and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/contrebia-belaisca">Contrebia Belaisca</a> in the Ebro valley. Their position between the Iberian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast and the interior made them important to every power that sought to control Spain.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/turiaso"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/turiaso.jpg" alt="Turiaso" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/turiaso">Turiaso</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vista_de_Tarazona,_Espa%C3%B1a,_2015-01-02,_DD_07.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Celtiberian economy rested on stock-raising and cereal farming on the cold, high plateau. Sheep, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and horses were the main wealth, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> describes Celtiberians wearing rough black woollen cloaks. Iron was mined in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moncayo-massif">Moncayo</a> range and around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilbilis">Bilbilis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turiaso">Turiaso</a>, and Celtiberian smiths were renowned: Diodorus says they buried iron plates until the weaker parts rusted away and forged weapons from what remained. Society was dominated by warrior elites bound to followers by clientage, and some followers were said to pledge their lives to a leader in a bond modern scholars call the <em>devotio iberica</em>. Agreements between individuals and communities were sealed by pacts of hospitality, recorded on small bronze tokens, the <em>tesserae hospitales</em>, often shaped as animals or clasped hands. Religion is known from inscriptions and classical reports; the rock inscriptions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penalba-de-villastar">Peñalba de Villastar</a> name the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugus">Lugus</a>, also worshipped by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> elsewhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silius-italicus">Silius Italicus</a> and Aelian report that warriors killed in battle were left to be eaten by vultures, believed to carry them to the heavens. In the second and first centuries BCE the Celtiberians adapted the Iberian script to write their own language, as on the bronze tablets from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botorrita">Botorrita</a>, the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/contrebia-belaisca">Contrebia Belaisca</a>, and many towns minted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> denarii and bronze coins bearing a horseman, used largely to pay for Roman taxes and troops.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-great-plains"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-great-plains.jpg" alt="Battle of the Great Plains" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-great-plains">Battle of the Great Plains</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Sulla_(loan_from_Ny_Carlsberg_Glyptotek)_-_Glyptothek_-_Munich_-_Germany_2017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">José Luiz, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Celtiberian warriors were among the most respected in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. They fought as infantry with long or short double-edged swords, daggers, spears and the all-iron javelin, and with large oblong shields or small round ones, and their cavalry was noted for dismounting to fight on foot when needed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> and later writers praised the quality of their swords, and the Roman <em>gladius hispaniensis</em> is generally thought to derive from Iberian, perhaps Celtiberian, models. Celtiberians served widely as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>: in 211 BCE some 20,000 hired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> deserted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-cornelius-scipio-calvus">Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio</a>, contributing to the destruction of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio</a> brothers' armies, and about 4,000 fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-great-plains">Battle of the Great Plains</a> in 203 BCE. Their wars with Rome followed. The First Celtiberian War of 181–179 BCE ended with treaties made by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> Sempronius Gracchus. In 153 BCE, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segeda">Segeda</a> began new walls, the Belli under their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carus-of-segeda">Carus</a> ambushed the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-fulvius-nobilior">Quintus Fulvius Nobilior</a> and, according to Appian, killed about 6,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>; the war, which Polybius called a fiery war, ended in 151 BCE. In the Numantine War of 143–133 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arevaci">Arevaci</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numantia">Numantia</a> defeated several Roman commanders and in 137 BCE forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-hostilius-mancinus">Gaius Hostilius Mancinus</a> to surrender with about 20,000 men. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus">Scipio Aemilianus</a> finally surrounded the city with a ring of camps and walls and starved it into surrender in 133 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rome"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rome.jpg" alt="Rome" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trevi_Fountain,_Rome,_Italy_2_-_May_2007.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diliff, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Celtiberians had no common government. Each people was made up of towns and their territories, and each town managed its own affairs through a council of elders and an assembly of armed men, with elected magistrates and, in wartime, chosen commanders. Appian describes Numantine envoys negotiating with Roman generals and the Belli electing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carus-of-segeda">Carus of Segeda</a> as their general in 153 BCE, and in the town of Belgeda around 92 BCE the people burned their council house with its councillors inside during a dispute over rebellion against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Alliances between towns were frequent but unstable; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arevaci">Arevaci</a> and Belli made common cause in 153 BCE, while other communities sided with Rome. The great bronze inscription known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botorrita">Botorrita</a> I, of around 100 BCE, seems to set out regulations for a sacred area and lists named officials of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/contrebia-belaisca">Contrebia Belaisca</a>, though its interpretation remains uncertain. A second bronze from the same site, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabula-contrebiensis">Tabula Contrebiensis</a></em> of 87 BCE, records a land and water dispute between neighbouring communities decided by the senate of Contrebia under the authority of the Roman governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-valerius-flaccus">Gaius Valerius Flaccus</a>, showing local institutions working within Roman provincial rule. The treaties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> Sempronius Gracchus in 179 BCE fixed obligations for many towns, including tribute, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auxilia">auxiliary troops</a> and a ban on founding new fortified towns.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/botorrita"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/botorrita.jpg" alt="Botorrita" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/botorrita">Botorrita</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cielo_07.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vikyto, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Celtiberians shaped the Roman conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, which took some two centuries largely because of resistance on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meseta-central">Meseta</a>. Their wars changed Roman practice: the start of the consular year was moved to 1 January in 153 BCE, a change traditionally linked to the urgency of the Celtiberian war, and the disgrace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-hostilius-mancinus">Mancinus</a> in 137 BCE, whose treaty had been negotiated by the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> Sempronius Gracchus, contributed to the political crisis of the following years at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Their swordsmithing influenced Roman equipment. Their language, written in the Celtiberian script and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> letters, is among the earliest and best documented ancient Celtic languages, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botorrita">Botorrita</a> bronzes are key texts for the study of Celtic. After Romanisation, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celtiberia">Celtiberia</a> produced Latin writers: the poet Martial, born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilbilis">Bilbilis</a> near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calatayud">Calatayud</a>, described himself as sprung from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a> and praised the iron of his home town. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numantia">Numantia</a>'s last stand became a symbol of heroic resistance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-de-cervantes">Miguel de Cervantes</a> dramatised it in <em>El cerco de Numancia</em>, the siege became a theme of Spanish national identity, and the site was declared a national monument in 1882. The excavations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-schulten">Adolf Schulten</a> from 1905 uncovered the Roman siege camps around the town, which remain among the best known Roman siege works anywhere.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sertorian-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sertorian-war.jpg" alt="Sertorian War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sertorian-war">Sertorian War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sertorian_war.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jaume Noguera, Pau Valdés, and Eduard Ble, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numantia">Numantia</a> in 133 BCE broke Celtiberian independence. According to Appian, many defenders killed themselves rather than surrender, the survivors were sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> except fifty kept for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus">Scipio Aemilianus</a>' triumph, and the town was razed and its land divided among its neighbours. Resistance continued for another two generations. Revolts in the 90s BCE were put down by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-didius">Titus Didius</a>, governor from 98 BCE, who moved the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiermes">Termes</a> down from their hilltop to an unfortified site, and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-valerius-flaccus">Gaius Valerius Flaccus</a>, who around 92 BCE dealt with the revolt in which Belgeda's council was burned. The final phase came in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sertorian-war">Sertorian War</a> of 80–72 BCE, when the Roman rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-sertorius">Quintus Sertorius</a> won the support of Lusitanians and Celtiberians and resisted the armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-pius">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> for several years. After Sertorius was assassinated, probably in 73 BCE, Pompey defeated his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-perperna-vento">Marcus Perperna</a> in 72 BCE and reduced the remaining Celtiberian towns, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uxama-argaela">Uxama</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clunia">Clunia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celtiberia">Celtiberia</a> was then fully absorbed into the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a>, later Tarraconensis. Its towns adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> and urban forms, places such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segobriga">Segobriga</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilbilis">Bilbilis</a> and Clunia flourished as Roman municipalities under Augustus and his successors, and the Celtiberian language and script disappeared from inscriptions by the first century CE.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Celtiberians matter as the Celtic peoples of inland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> whose warrior towns, iron weapons and fierce resistance made the Roman conquest of the peninsula a two-century struggle, culminating in the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numantia">Numantia</a>, and whose language, preserved on bronzes such as those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botorrita">Botorrita</a>, is a key witness to ancient Celtic.</em></p>

